What is an op-amp based comparator?

.   -Unless specified, the information below is from *(lectronicsTutorials)(https://www.electronics-tutorials.ws/opamp/op-amp-comparator.html)*

.   -An op-amp is an electronic component that has an inverting input \[-]nd a non inverting input \[+]

.   -An op-amp based comparator compares one analog voltage level with another analog voltage level

.   -Produces a voltage output based on the comparison of the two levels

.   -Basically, it finds which output is the largest magnitude

.       -Produces a negative \[low] value if the input level is less than the reference level

.       -Produces a positive \[high] value if the input level is greater than the reference level

How to use an op-amp based comparator:

. -Non-inverting op-amp circuit:

.   -When input voltage is greater than the reference voltage, then it sends out a high output

.   -When input voltage is lower than the reference voltage, then it sends out a low output

. -Inverting op-amp circuit:

.    -Does the opposite thing as the non-inverting op-amp circuit

. -Window op-amp circuit:

.    -Both of the previous circuits combined into one

.  -Has two reference voltages instead of one

.  -Input voltage rests outside two reference voltages

.  -If inside the two ref voltages, then high output. If outside the two reference voltages, then low output.

.-Voltage level detector:

.  -By connecting multiple op amps in a circuit, you can make a voltage level detector

.  -All share an input
